- **[B047]**            **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** DNSCanaries.swift: expired RRSIGs on the known-good control now emit `.inconclusive` (clock-unreliable) instead of `.positive` (threat). DNSSECVerifier already has B047 span guard (`maxPlausibleValiditySpanSeconds`).
- **Criticality:**      Critical; a spoofing verdict driven by an attacker-controllable datum (the
                        local clock).
- **Location:**         macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/DNSSECVerifier.swift:77, 100
- **Issue:**            RRSIG validity is judged against the local wall clock; a clock skew flips
                        the target and control canary to expired simultaneously → two 1.2 spoofing
                        supports → belief ~0.91 → a "spoofing" verdict produced purely from an
                        attacker-controllable (or benignly-wrong) clock.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** Related to B050 (target expired RRSIG treated as attack).
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Corroborate a trusted time source or bound the skew → inconclusive.
                        [Fix 2] Never render expired-from-unverified-clock as a threat.
- **Tag:**              CONFIRMED

                       - **Tool Name**            [Claude]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-11
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       N/A
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Critical is correct. The B046 fix will make RRSIG
                                                  appear on real DNS, making B047 more acute: once the
                                                  DO bit is set, clock-skew-driven expiry will fire as
                                                  a real verdict signal. Fix 2 (never render expired-
                                                  from-unverified-clock as a threat) is the higher-
                                                  priority fix; it addresses the root cause (attacker-
                                                  controllable input). Fix 1 (trusted time source) is
                                                  the ideal but harder solution. For Fix 2, the
                                                  recommended approach is: when both target and control
                                                  canary RRSIGs are expired simultaneously, emit
                                                  `.inconclusive` (clock unreliable) not `.positive`.

                       - **Tool Name**            [Codex]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/DNSSECVerifier.swift:77, 100
                                                  — Live-source inspection confirms the operative
                                                  behavior: RRSIG validity is judged against the
                                                  local wall clock; a clock skew flips the target
                                                  and control canary to expired simultaneously → two
                                                  1.2 spoofing supports → belief ~0.91 → a
                                                  "spoofing" verdict produced purely from an
                                                  attacker-controllable (or benignly-wrong) clock.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Keep in B-domain; required disposition: [Fix 1]
                                                  Corroborate a trusted time source or bound the
                                                  skew → inconclusive. [Fix 2] Never render
                                                  expired-from-unverified-clock as a threat.

                       - **Tool Name**            [Claude]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       Confirmed: checkStatus takes now: Date = Date()
                                                  (DNSSECVerifier.swift:77) and anyCurrentlyValid
                                                  tests now within [inception, expiration]
                                                  (DNSSECVerifier.swift:100); a skewed/attacker-set
                                                  local clock flips both target and control RRSIGs to
                                                  expired -> .signatureExpiredOrNotYetValid ->
                                                  dnssecCanaryExpired positive. A verdict from an
                                                  attacker-controllable datum. Critical. The B046 fix
                                                  makes this acute because RRSIGs then actually
                                                  appear on real DNS.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Fix 2 first (root cause): when target AND control
                                                  both read expired simultaneously, emit
                                                  .inconclusive (clock unreliable), not a threat.
                                                  Fix 1 (bounded skew vs a trusted time source) is
                                                  the durable answer. Sequence AFTER B046. Priority:
                                                  high.


                        - **Tool Name**            [opencode]
                        - **Date**                 2026-07-19
                        - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                        - **Disagree Basis**       Verified against live source code. Refer to delta description for specific code paths and line numbers.
