- **[D020]**            Name-Decompression O(n^2) on a Hostile TCP Response — CPU DoS
- **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Added global pointer-jump budget (1024 jumps) across all parseName calls per response, and capped answer count at 256 to prevent CPU DoS.
- **Criticality:**      Non-critical (Med); a hostile TC-forced ~64KB TCP response burns sub-second-
                        to-seconds CPU, chainable.
- **Location:**         macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/DNSResolver.swift:737-772, 949-994 (via sendTCP:1078)
- **Issue:**            Name-decompression is O(n^2) on a hostile ~64KB TCP response (TC-forced)
                        packed with pointer-laden names → hundreds of millions of label visits, sub-
                        second-to-seconds CPU, chainable across requests. The existing bounds
                        prevent OOM but not CPU exhaustion.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** Not previously tracked.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Cap total pointer-jumps per response.
                        [Fix 2] Cap answer count (e.g., 256).
- **Tag:**              LIKELY

                       - **Tool Name**            [Codex]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/DNSResolver.swift:737-772,
                                                  949-994 (via sendTCP:1078) — Production-source
                                                  inspection shows: Name-decompression is O(n^2) on
                                                  a hostile ~64KB TCP response (TC-forced) packed
                                                  with pointer-laden names → hundreds of millions of
                                                  label visits, sub- second-to-seconds CPU,
                                                  chainable across requests. The existing bounds
                                                  prevent OOM but not CPU exhaustion.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Retain the delta and its proposed fixes; require a
                                                  real production-path run and an adversarial or
                                                  failure case before closure.

                       - **Tool Name**            [Claude]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       Verified: parseName (:949-994) has a per-call
                                                  visited Set that bounds ONE name to
                                                  O(bytes.count), but the answer loop (:737)
                                                  iterates up to ~thousands of records (min
                                                  ~12B/record -> ~5400 in a 64KB TCP response),
                                                  each re-parsing pointer-laden names -> ~hundreds
                                                  of millions of label/offset visits. Existing
                                                  bounds stop OOM and compression loops, not
                                                  aggregate CPU.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Agree (Med, CPU DoS). Fix 1 (a single global
                                                  pointer-jump budget across the whole response)
                                                  is the robust cap; an answer-count cap (Fix 2)
                                                  alone does not bound per-name cost. Validate
                                                  with a crafted 64KB TC-forced response of
                                                  pointer-dense names; measure wall-clock.

