### E. Platform Integration
---
---
- **[E037]**            **NON-CODEABLE** Requires Windows Filtering Platform (WFP) callout driver or equivalent kernel-level packet filter — substantial driver development outside the scope of a minimal source fix.
                        Windows Raw Socket Captures All IP Traffic — No Kernel-Level Packet Filter
- **Criticality:**      Medium; all IP traffic is captured and processed in userspace, causing
                        performance degradation and potential DNS packet loss under backpressure.
- **Location:**         Windows/SyxxCage/Program.cs:219-223 (IOControlCode.ReceiveAll);
                        Windows/SyxxCage/Program.cs:99-104 (userspace port-53 filter);
                        macOS/Sources/SyxxCage/SyxxCage.swift:117 (BPF "udp port 53" filter);
                        macOS/Sources/Ccage/Ccage.c:65-78 (pcap_compile + pcap_setfilter).
- **Issue:**            [CONFIRMED] Windows capture uses raw sockets with
                        IOControlCode.ReceiveAll (Program.cs:223), which captures ALL IP packets
                        regardless of protocol or port. The port-53 check at line 104
                        (`context.SourcePort != 53 && context.DestPort != 53`) filters in
                        userspace after the full packet is read from the socket. On macOS, the
                        BPF filter `"udp port 53"` (SyxxCage.swift:117) is compiled and applied
                        at the kernel level (Ccage.c:65-78), so only UDP port-53 packets ever
                        reach userspace. The consequences on Windows are: (a) the socket buffer
                        fills with non-DNS IP packets (HTTP, TLS, ICMP, etc.), creating
                        backpressure that can cause incoming DNS packets to be dropped by the
                        kernel when the receive buffer is full; (b) every non-DNS packet is
                        copied from kernel to userspace and parsed through ParseIPUDPHeader
                        before being discarded at line 104 — wasted CPU on a path that should
                        never see non-DNS traffic; (c) on a busy network, the capture loop
                        spends most of its time processing irrelevant packets, reducing the
                        effective capture rate for actual DNS traffic.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** Not related to any known delta. D031 concerns attribution timing,
                        not capture filtering.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Use a Windows Filtering Platform (WFP) callout or a BPF-
                        equivalent to restrict capture to UDP port 53 at the kernel level,
                        mirroring macOS behavior.
                        [Fix 2] As a minimal fix, read from the socket in a dedicated thread
                        with a larger buffer to reduce backpressure, and move the port-53 check
                        earlier in the receive path.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Windows/SyxxCage/Program.cs:219-223
                        socket.SetSocketOption(SocketOptionLevel.IP,
                            SocketOptionName.HeaderIncluded,
                            family == AddressFamily.InterNetwork);
                        byte[] byIn = new byte[] { 1, 0, 0, 0 };
                        byte[] byOut = new byte[] { 1, 0, 0, 0 };
                        socket.IOControl(IOControlCode.ReceiveAll, byIn, byOut);
                        // ALL IP traffic captured — no kernel filter

                        // macOS/Sources/SyxxCage/SyxxCage.swift:117 (correct)
                        guard cage_pcap_compile_filter(pcap, "udp port 53", 1, 0,
                                                       &errbuf) == 0 else {
                        // Kernel-level BPF filter — only UDP/53 reaches userspace

                        ]
