- **[D056]**            **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** `encryptedDNSStatus` now requires ALL scoped resolvers to have encrypted protocols before returning `.yes`; mixed configs return `.no` naming the leaking resolvers. Both macOS and Windows.
                        Encrypted DNS Status Evaluates to Yes on Mixed Encrypted/Unencrypted Resolvers on Both Platforms
- **Criticality:**      Critical; a security false-confidence verdict that reports DNS is encrypted when plaintext leaks are active.
- **Location:**         macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/NetworkKeyState.swift:385-392;
                        Windows/Syxx.Kit/NetworkKeyState.cs:327-337
- **Issue:**            [CONFIRMED] `encryptedDNSStatus` evaluates system DNS transport. On macOS, it loops through scoped resolvers: if any resolver has an encrypted protocol, it returns `.yes`. On Windows, it loops through servers: if any server has a configured DoH template, it returns `.yes`. If a machine has mixed resolvers (e.g. one encrypted DoH resolver and one plain UDP resolver), queries will leak in plaintext, but the status claims the transport is secure.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B032 covers VPN tunnel interfaces, but not this mixed-resolver validation escape.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Ensure all active/configured resolvers are encrypted to return `.yes`; otherwise return `.no` or a degraded state.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Windows/Syxx.Kit/NetworkKeyState.cs:327-337
                        foreach (var server in servers) {
                            if (HasConfiguredDoHTemplate(server)) {
                                return new EncryptedDNSStatus { State = TriState.Yes, ... };
                                // ^^ immediately returns Yes if ANY server matches
                            }
                        }

                        ]
