- **[E046]**            **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code), already resolved.** The old netstat/findstr shell-out has been replaced with in-process `GetExtendedUdpTable` P/Invoke (DNSCapture.cs:497-530), which reads the kernel's UDP owner table directly.
                        Windows DNS Capture Process Attribution Always Fails Due to Incorrect netstat Regex Search
- **Criticality:**      High
- **Location:**         Windows/Syxx.Kit/DNSCapture.cs:494
- **Issue:**            [CONFIRMED] Windows process resolution shells out to `netstat -ano | findstr /R ":{port} .*UDP"`. In standard netstat output, the protocol string `"UDP"` appears at the beginning of the line (before the port), so the search regex pattern `":{port} .*UDP"` will never match, causing all process lookups to return `Unknown`.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** None.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Update the findstr regex pattern to correctly match the netstat layout or query the IP helper tables directly using `GetExtendedUdpTable` instead of spawning shell processes.
- **Tag:**              CONFIRMED


                       - **Tool Name**            [Codex]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       Windows/Syxx.Kit/DNSCapture.cs:487-523 runs
                                                  `netstat -ano | findstr /R ":{port} .*UDP"`; UDP
                                                  normally precedes the endpoint, so the pattern can
                                                  lose PID attribution.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Use GetExtendedUdpTable and return unknown on
                                                  attribution failure.


                       - **Tool Name**            [Claude]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       Confirmed DNSCapture.cs:494: netstat -ano |
                                                  findstr /R ":{port} .*UDP". In netstat output
                                                  'UDP' precedes the local address (e.g. `UDP
                                                  0.0.0.0:53 *:* 1234`), so requiring UDP AFTER
                                                  ':port ' never matches -> every UDP lookup
                                                  returns ProcessInfoReport.Unknown (fails safe,
                                                  no fabricated PID). macOS uses pktap and works;
                                                  Windows Cage process attribution is entirely
                                                  dead.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Replace the shell/findstr with
                                                  GetExtendedUdpTable (P/Invoke), returning
                                                  Unknown on miss; verify a real run attributes a
                                                  live query PID before closure. High: a whole
                                                  Cage datapoint is broken on Windows — confirm
                                                  downstream treats Unknown as inconclusive, not
                                                  benign.
