- **[C031]**            Collector Protocol Calls Every Successful Recursive Lookup a DNS Leak
- **Criticality:**      Critical; routine functioning DNS produces false “LEAK CONFIRMED,” while
                        the protocol has no defensible no-leak semantics.
- **Location:**         Server/syxx_server/leak.py:138-151;
                        macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/LeakDetector.swift:277-292;
                        Windows/Syxx.Kit/LeakDetector.cs:312-329
- **Issue:**            The test deliberately asks system resolvers to recurse for a unique token.
                        Server logic then defines `leaked = observedResolvers.count > 0`, and clients
                        merely check Boolean/list consistency. They never compare observations with
                        an authenticated expected VPN/tunnel resolver or egress policy. Any working
                        resolver therefore confirms a “leak,” not an unexpected path outside a tunnel.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** A024/C026 cover the opposite zero-observation false-clean branch. This is the
                        structural false-positive branch and shows the current protocol is ineffective.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Attest observation/liveness separately. [Fix 2] Confirm a leak only
                        against a trustworthy expected/allowed resolver or egress set. [Fix 3]
                        Without that policy, return observations plus inconclusive—not a leak verdict.
- **Tag:**              CONFIRMED — FOUNDATIONAL DEFECT


                       - **Tool Name**            [Codex]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       Server/syxx_server/leak.py:138-151;
                                                  macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/LeakDetector.swift:277-292;
                                                  Windows/Syxx.Kit/LeakDetector.cs:312-329 —
                                                  Live-source inspection confirms the operative
                                                  behavior: The test deliberately asks system
                                                  resolvers to recurse for a unique token. Server
                                                  logic then defines `leaked =
                                                  observedResolvers.count > 0`, and clients merely
                                                  check Boolean/list consistency. They never compare
                                                  observations with an authenticated expected
                                                  VPN/tunnel resolver or egress policy. Any working
                                                  resolver therefore confirms a “leak,” not an
                                                  unexpected path outside a tunnel.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Keep in C-domain; required disposition: [Fix 1]
                                                  Attest observation/liveness separately. [Fix 2]
                                                  Confirm a leak only against a trustworthy
                                                  expected/allowed resolver or egress set. [Fix 3]
                                                  Without that policy, return observations plus
                                                  inconclusive—not a leak verdict.


                       - **Tool Name**            [Claude]
                       - **Date**                 2026-07-13
                       - **Agree / Disagree**     Agree
                       - **Disagree Basis**       Confirmed leak.py:144 leaked=len(resolvers)>0;
                                                  ReconcileVerdict (LeakDetector.swift:283-292 /
                                                  .cs:321-328) returns .confirmed for any non-empty
                                                  observed set. No comparison to an expected/allowed
                                                  VPN/tunnel resolver or egress policy exists
                                                  anywhere, so any working recursive resolver
                                                  "confirms a leak." Codex correct; structural, not a
                                                  bug.
                       - **Proposed Revisions**   Agree. Priority: Critical/foundational (systemic
                                                  false-positive; no defensible no-leak semantics).
                                                  Fixes 1-3 sound; note the mapping difficulty:
                                                  authoritative-observed egress IPs are
                                                  recursive-server egresses, not the client's
                                                  configured resolver, so "expected set" derivation
                                                  is nontrivial. Until solved, Fix 3 (observations +
                                                  inconclusive, never a leak verdict) is the only
                                                  honest default and should ship first.

                       **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** reconcileResult now returns inconclusive (not confirmed) when leaked is true but no expected-resolver baseline is configured; observations are surfaced as evidence without a misleading leak verdict.


