## Verified-clean in scope (checked, no defect found)

- `stripe_checkout.verify_webhook` (:109-136) — constant-time compare over `t.payload`, replay window
  enforced, and `json.loads` runs **after** verification. `app.py:942-948` calls it before any
  fulfilment side effect. Correct order, no finding.
- `stripe_checkout.create_checkout_session` (:63-106) — explicit price id, archived-price refusal,
  `mode` derived from the live price object rather than hardcoded. No fabricated price path.
- `licensing._verify_validate_key_response` (:119-229) — fails closed without a public key or without
  `cryptography` outside dev mode; the digest used in the signing string is **recomputed from the body**
  rather than trusted from the header; A056 date-skew bound is real and applied before the verify.
- `licensing._keygen_sem` (:249-250, :286-287, :351-352, :369-370) — acquire sits outside the `try` in
  both users, so the `finally: release()` cannot over-release on acquisition failure.
- `db.monitor_delete` (:888-904) — both statements are subject-scoped and the real `rowcount` is
  returned. `db.api_key_revoke` (:748-762) likewise. (`api_key_revoke_all_for_subject` is **not** — see
  S3-03.)
- `db.monitor_advance` (:834-852) — the S15 connection leak is genuinely fixed; one connection, one
  `finally: close()`.
- `db.purge_expired` (:907-964) — runs as a single `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction, so it cannot partially
  apply; the S16 `DELETE FROM t AS x` syntax hazard is genuinely avoided at :921-924.
- `passivedns.query` (:18-26) — pops `resolver` at the API boundary as A023 requires.
- Email header injection via `beta_reserve` — not reachable: `EmailMessage.__setitem__` raises on CR/LF
  (verified by execution), and `app.py:1161` applies `_EMAIL_RE` first. The *raise* is the S3-10 issue,
  not an injection.

**[A189]**
**Criticality:** CRITICAL
**Location:** `Server/leak-test.html:150-154`, reinforced by static copy at `Server/leak-test.html:99-103` and `Server/leak-test.html:66`
**Issue:** The page renders a **definitive security verdict from a count of unique resolver IPs**. `applyVerdict()` maps `count <= 2` to the green "safe" style with the headline **"No leak detected"** and the body text "Your VPN or configured resolver appears to be handling all lookups." The authoritative server observes only the *last-mile recursive resolver IP*. From that datum it is structurally impossible to determine (a) whether the client→resolver hop was encrypted (DoH/DoT/plaintext are indistinguishable at the authoritative), (b) whether the observed resolver belongs to the user's VPN or to their ISP, or (c) whether any query escaped a tunnel. A user behind their ISP's resolver with no VPN at all sees exactly one resolver IP and is told "No leak detected" in green — the maximum-confidence reassuring label produced on evidence that cannot support it. Symmetrically, `count >= 5` prints "DNS leak detected … your browsing activity is partially exposed" when the same observation is fully explained by a resolver pool doing load balancing (Cloudflare, Google, and Quad9 all egress from many IPs). Both poles are unearned. Direct violation of honesty spine #1 (no verdict before evidence) and #4 (no fabricated value shown as measured). The sibling page `leaktest.html:253-274` already carries the C076 hedge comment and hedged wording for the identical measurement — this page was left un-hedged.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** No delta ID matches. Grep of `DELTAS.md`, `DELTAS-fixed.md`, `DELTAS-rejected.md`, `Server/SERVER_DELTAS.md` returns **zero hits for `C076`** and zero hits for `leak-test`/`leaktest`. The C076 remediation exists only as a source comment in `leaktest.html:253-254`; it was never applied to `leak-test.html` and is not tracked in any delta file. **NEW — file a delta.**
**Fix(es):**
1. Delete `applyVerdict()`'s tri-band verdict entirely and adopt the hedged descriptive form already written for C076 in `leaktest.html:263-273`: state the observation ("N resolver(s) forwarded your queries"), never a verdict, and instruct the user to compare the IPs against their VPN/DoH provider.
2. Remove the green `v-safe` class from any resolver-count outcome; the palette itself is a verdict. Reserve teal for states actually proven.
3. Rewrite the static interpretation list at `leak-test.html:99-103` — "1–2 resolvers … No leak detected" is the same unearned claim in prose and will be read even when the test is never run.
4. Fix the header claim at `leak-test.html:66` ("If multiple resolvers appear, your DNS traffic may be leaking outside your VPN or tunnel") to state the actual limitation: the test observes last-mile resolvers only and cannot see encryption status or tunnel membership.
**Code Sample:**
```
150	function applyVerdict(count) {
151	  if (count <= 2) setVerdict('v-safe', 'No leak detected', `Your DNS queries resolved through ${count} resolver${count===1?'':'s'}. Your VPN or configured resolver appears to be handling all lookups.`);
152	  else if (count <= 4) setVerdict('v-warn', 'Possible DNS leak', `${count} distinct resolvers observed. Some queries may be bypassing your VPN. Investigate your DNS configuration.`);
153	  else setVerdict('v-leak', 'DNS leak detected', `${count} distinct resolvers observed. Your DNS traffic is splitting across multiple paths — your browsing activity is partially exposed.`);
154	}
```
```
 99	    <ul>
100	      <li><strong>1–2 resolvers</strong> — All queries flow through a single path (your VPN or configured resolver). No leak detected.</li>
101	      <li><strong>3–4 resolvers</strong> — Queries are splitting across multiple paths. This is a possible leak worth investigating further.</li>
102	      <li><strong>5+ resolvers</strong> — Queries are reaching many different resolvers. A DNS leak is very likely, and your browsing activity is partially exposed.</li>
103	    </ul>
```

