### Files with no findings

None — every file in scope produced at least one finding, except `SyxxMatrixIntegration.swift`, whose
only defects are the spec-side halves of the coverage finding above (its own plumbing —
`toSectionResult`/`toObservedIndicator`/`SyxxRunInput` — is faithful and lossless).

- **[B177]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code), already resolved.** D011 added trust-set passing at all production call sites (SyxxWitness.swift verify/report paths and AppModel.swift GUI verify both pass `CollectorPinning.configuredTrustedSigningKeys()`). CLI already renders distinct "SIGNATURE VALID — unpinned self-attestation" when no trust anchors configured. Remaining concern (trust set often empty because syncTrustedSigningKey had no callers) addressed by B185.
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         WitnessSigner.swift:205-209; callers SyxxWitness.swift:185,208; AppModel.swift:1018,1569
- **Issue:**            `.untrustedSigner` only returns when caller passes non-empty `trustedPublicKeys`. NO production caller does — only tests. Dead state in shipped product. Snapshot signed with attacker keypair (attacker pubkey embedded in same file) verifies `.valid`; CLI prints "✅ VALID (Tamper-evident — Ed25519 signed)". LeakDetector added a default trust source (LeakDetector.swift:160); WitnessSigner has no equivalent.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** D011 (DELTAS.md:2383), B081 (DELTAS.md:14039). DELTAS-fixed.md:13238 already records this as still true.
- **Fix(es):**          Default trust source mirroring LeakDetector.swift:160; until configured, render a distinct 4th state "SIGNATURE VALID — unpinned self-attestation"; add `--trust` flag; test the SHIPPED path rejects a foreign key.

- **[B178]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code), already resolved.** B254 added verify-before-render: `report` command now calls `verifySnapshotDetailed` and refuses to render unless `.valid`. Failed verification prints `printReportRefusal` with no snapshot content and returns non-zero exit.
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         SyxxWitness.swift:248-261; SignedIncidentReport.swift:30-133 (claim at :127-129)
- **Issue:**            Decodes JSON then renders. NO verifySnapshot/verifySnapshotDetailed/Curve25519 call on this path. Prints verdict, answer sets, key ID, signature prefix, then "⚠ Tamper-evident: altering any data above will break the Ed25519 signature." Attacker-supplied JSON with garbage signature prints under that banner. This is the shareable artifact the product's value rests on.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** Related B135 (DELTAS.md:1488); the verification gap itself is UNFILED.
- **Fix(es):**          render() must take a verified SnapshotVerification and refuse a verdict block for anything but .valid; golden vector: corrupted signature must not produce "Tamper-evident".

- **[B179]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** SignedIncidentReport.render now uses the typed outcome fields (`localOutcome.records`, `publicResolverOutcomes`) for the emptiness check — these are covered by the display-integrity guard. Legacy `localRecords`/`publicResolverResults` are no longer used for the conclusion gate.
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         SignedIncidentReport.swift:63-64,74-75; VantageComparer.swift:40-41; SignedEvidencePayload.swift:124-137,149-153; WitnessSigner.swift:212-230
- **Issue:**            ComparisonResult carries legacy `localRecords`/`publicResolverResults` alongside `localOutcome`/`publicResolverOutcomes`. SignedEvidencePayload.build reads only the latter; the D083 display guard compares only queryId/comparisonResult/resolverQueryOutcomes/timestamps/formatVersion. The legacy pair is covered by NOTHING, and the report gates its top-level verdict on exactly those. Editing JSON to empty them flips a DIVERGES report to "INCONCLUSIVE — Nothing was compared" with the seal intact. Same class for isDegraded/isInconclusive (both map to canonical "inconclusive").
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B135 introduced the guard; B037/D083 is the mechanism that fails to cover it.
- **Fix(es):**          Extend display guard to the legacy fields; better — render exclusively from `snapshot.evidence` (the signed DTO). Port to Syxx.Kit. Golden vector for emptied localRecords → .displayMismatch.

- **[B180]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Added `extractSignedObservedResolvers` to extract the resolver list from the signed payload. All return paths and `reconcileResult` now use the signed resolver list; the unsigned outer field is only a fallback when the signed payload is missing the field.
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         LeakDetector.swift:214-258,286-300,363-371; Server/syxx_server/leak.py:234-248
- **Issue:**            verify() checks Ed25519 over an opaque blob it never parses. Verdict computed from outer `result.leaked`/`result.observedResolvers`; observedResolvers surfaced from that unsigned copy. A relay preserving signedPayload/signature byte-for-byte while rewriting outer observedResolvers yields green "✅ signature valid" beside a forged resolver list. Verdict is additionally never gated on signatureValid — .confirmed/.notDetected returned with signatureValid == false.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B091 (DELTAS.md:20006) exactly this, still open; D004 (DELTAS.md:2012).
- **Fix(es):**          Decode signedPayload into a strict DTO; derive verdict+resolvers only from it; outer/inner mismatch → .inconclusive; force .inconclusive when signatureValid != true. Twin in Syxx.Kit.

- **[B181]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** `extractSignedLeaked` now derives `leaked` from `queriesObserved` + `observedResolvers` in the signed payload when the `leaked` key is absent (forward-compatible if server adds it). Collector leak tests no longer always return .inconclusive.
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         LeakDetector.swift:214-232,363-371; leak.py:206-248; app.py:1486-1497
- **Issue:**            VerifyResponse declares `leaked: Bool?`. Server body has token/zone/issuedAt/queriesObserved/resolverCount/observedResolvers/observations/observationsAttested/verifiedAt — no `leaked` key anywhere in Server/. So `leaked` always decodes nil, guard `result.inconclusive == true || result.leaked == nil` is ALWAYS true, always returns hardcoded reason "Collector recorded no DNS queries for this token within the verification window." even when observedResolvers is non-empty and signed. reconcileVerdict/expectedResolvers/.confirmed/.notDetected are UNREACHABLE in production. CLI prints INCONCLUSIVE + the resolver list + "✅ signature VALID" simultaneously.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** No delta filed. Documented as root cause in DELTA_FIX_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md:142.
- **Fix(es):**          Derive verdict from signed queriesObserved+observedResolvers (closes B091 too); never emit a fixed reason the data can contradict; contract test with a real server fixture; version the payload so a rename fails loudly.

- **[B182]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code), already resolved.** Current code uses `guard let payload = try? snapshot.evidence.canonicalBytes() else { return .malformedInput }` — canonicalization failure correctly returns `.malformedInput`, not empty bytes.
- **Criticality:**      High
- **Location:**         WitnessSigner.swift:236-240
- **Issue:**            `let payload = (try? snapshot.evidence.canonicalBytes()) ?? Data()`. On encoder failure the verifier asks whether the signature is valid for the EMPTY message. The typed .malformedInput exists two lines above and is unused. Combined with finding 1 (no trust set), an attacker who can force a throw supplies their own key plus a signature over Data() and obtains .valid. Sign side correctly propagates the throw (:121).
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** Not filed. Adjacent E021 (DELTAS.md:10392).
- **Fix(es):**          `guard let payload = try? ... else { return .malformedInput }`. Audit Syxx.Kit twin for the same catch-and-default.

- **[B183]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** `createSignedSnapshot` now applies a non-nil default expiry (30 days) when caller passes `expiresAt: nil`. Combined with D005's verifier-side defaultMaxAge, every snapshot has a finite lifetime. The `defaultExpiry` constant matches the verifier's `defaultMaxAge`.
- **Criticality:**      High
- **Location:**         WitnessSigner.swift:99,152,175-180,188-196; WitnessTestifyPlanner.swift:51-52; SyxxWitness.swift:81,102; AppModel.swift:997,1018,1569
- **Issue:**            createSignedSnapshot defaults expiresAt nil; every production signer takes the default (CLI has no --expires flag). So .expired can never fire on a Syxx-produced snapshot. Symmetrically both GUI verify paths pass no maxAge. .expired/.stale states reachable only via hand-crafted input. An old snapshot of a then-clean network is indefinitely replayable as current evidence.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** D005 (DELTAS.md:2109); DELTAS-fixed.md:13223 already records this unchanged.
- **Fix(es):**          Non-nil default expiry + --expires flag; default maxAge passed from both GUI sites; surface evidence age from evidence.timestamp. Parity in Syxx.Kit.

- **[B184]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Reordered `verifySnapshotDetailed`: signature verification and display-integrity check now execute BEFORE any time-based check (.expired/.stale/.futureDated). A forged snapshot with a past expiresAt now returns .invalidSignature, not .expired — no verdict before evidence.
- **Criticality:**      Medium-High
- **Location:**         WitnessSigner.swift:187-201 vs 232-239; SyxxWitness.swift:278-283; AppModel.swift:1574-1582
- **Issue:**            .expired/.stale/.futureDated evaluated against snapshot.evidence.* before any key parse or signature check. Those timestamps are only trustworthy AFTER the signature validates. A wholly fabricated file with a past expiresAt returns .expired and the CLI prints "❌ EXPIRED — The snapshot's expiry date has passed" — asserting the file is an authentic-but-lapsed Syxx artifact. Verdict before evidence; the verifier's own doc comment (:62-63) states the distinction it destroys.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** E021 established the states; the ordering defect is UNFILED.
- **Fix(es):**          Reorder: parse key → signature → display integrity → THEN time checks. Test: forged snapshot with past expiresAt must return .invalidSignature.

- **[B185]**               **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** `syncTrustedSigningKey` now uses the SPKI-pinned session (not URLSession.shared), validates 32-byte Ed25519 key + keyId SHA-256 prefix match, surfaces key rotation as a diagnostic event, and is called on app launch in `checkStartup()`.
- **Criticality:**      High
- **Location:**         LeakDetector.swift:493-504,516-541,546-548,105-112,160
- **Issue:**            configuredTrustedSigningKeys falls back to a UserDefaults value written by syncTrustedSigningKey, which fetches with URLSession.shared — NOT makeCollectorSession(pins:) built for this host — and persists whatever publicKey the response carries: no pin, no fingerprint confirmation, no comparison to a previously-persisted key (silent rotation). Whoever can present a valid chain for the collector host — the precise adversary this product detects — defines the trust set. Separately syncTrustedSigningKey has NO CALLERS anywhere, so the fallback never populates and signatureKeyTrusted is nil on every real run.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** D003 (DELTAS.md:1946), D004, D011; ROADMAP WS3-01/02 reopened at DELTAS-fixed.md:13238.
- **Fix(es):**          Fetch through the pinned session; refuse to persist when pins empty; validate 32-byte Ed25519 + keyId matches SHA-256 prefix; surface key rotation rather than silently replacing; actually call it on launch or delete it.

- **[B186]**               Neither macOS surface renders signatureKeyTrusted; CLI collapses the tri-state — nil renders as "INVALID"
- **Criticality:**      High
- **Location:**         GluttonView.swift:212-222; SyxxLeak.swift:96-102; LeakDetector.swift:60-66,246-248
- **Issue:**            signatureKeyTrusted is a documented tri-state, computed but never rendered. GluttonView shows only signatureValid, green, never trust or transportPinning. CLI maps nil ("nothing was verified") onto "⚠️ Ed25519 signature INVALID" — an unmeasured state rendered as a definite forgery finding; and a crypto-valid signature from an UNTRUSTED key renders identically to a trusted one. transportPinning never printed, so unpinned is indistinguishable from pinned.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B066 (DELTAS.md:11926) GUI half CONFIRMED; CLI half tracks DELTAS-rejected.md:7801.
- **Fix(es):**          Four distinct states in both surfaces; print transportPinning + unconfigured caveat; colour only valid+trusted+pinned green. Windows twin.

**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** GluttonView already fixed; CLI now renders four typed states (valid+trusted, valid+untrusted, valid+unconfigured, invalid, no-key) and surfaces transport pinning.

- **[B187]**               macOS client trusts unsigned Keygen meta.valid, and collapses 401/404 into a definite .invalid that forfeits offline grace
- **Criticality:**      High
- **Location:**         LicenseManager.swift:409-423,271-273; compare Server/syxx_server/licensing.py:268
- **Issue:**            Returns .valid on meta.valid with NO verification of Keygen-Signature/Digest/Date headers, no freshness, no pinning — while the SERVER verifies exactly those. Anything presenting a chain for api.keygen.sh can mint or revoke Pro. Separately 401/404 → .invalid turns an UNAUTHENTICATED status (classic captive-portal/interception response) into "your key is bad", and .invalid is the one branch that deliberately skips E-7 offline grace — locking out a paying user precisely on the filtered network this product investigates.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** A021 (server twin, fixed); macOS client half open per DELTAS-fixed.md:13247. D003 covers missing Keygen pinning.
- **Fix(es):**          Verify Ed25519 response signature against pinned KEYGEN_PUBLIC_KEY before honouring meta.valid; unverifiable → .networkError never .valid; treat 401 as transient (grace applies). Windows twin.

**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Added verifyKeygenSignature() with pinned Ed25519 verification, date freshness check, and body digest; 401/404 now → .networkError (offline grace applies); unverifiable meta.valid → .networkError. Public key constant needs to be populated.

- **[B188]**               Keychain at-rest posture asserted but not established — kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain never set; LicenseManager sets no accessibility at all
- **Criticality:**      Medium
- **Location:**         WitnessKeyManager.swift:4-13,104-111; LicenseManager.swift:74-80,115-136
- **Issue:**            WitnessKeyManager sets kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly and the header claims the key "never persists in plaintext in app memory, crash dumps, or backups" — a claim kSecAttrAccessible does not make about memory or crash dumps. kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain appears NOWHERE in the repo, so these are file-based keychain items and the asserted property is unproven. LicenseManager omits kSecAttrAccessible entirely, so license key, trial state, anti-rollback max-time and fingerprint use default accessibility while the signing key does not — inconsistent, no rationale. Both cache secrets as String for the process lifetime.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** Not filed. Adjacent D015 (DELTAS.md:2578).
- **Fix(es):**          Set kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain on every query in both managers; apply the accessibility class to LicenseManager items; correct the header claim; hold key as Data and zero after signing.

**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Added kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain to all keychain queries in both WitnessKeyManager and LicenseManager; applied kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly to LicenseManager items for consistency; corrected header claim about memory/crash dump protection.

- **[B189]**               `syxx witness create` accepts the Ed25519 private key on argv; generated key file written before it is restricted
- **Criticality:**      Medium-High
- **Location:**         SyxxWitness.swift:53-57,66-71,23-33
- **Issue:**            --key/-k read directly from argv, falling back to SYXX_WITNESS_KEY. `ps -ww` exposes another process's arguments to the same user; the value lands in shell history and CI logs. --gen-keypair was hardened against stdout leakage (D086) but the CONSUMPTION path was not. Also: key written with .atomic then chmod 0600 afterwards, leaving a window at process umask; ~/.syxx created without explicit 0700. GUI path is clean by comparison (Keychain + SecureField cleared after save).
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** RET-007, recorded NOT fixed at DELTAS-fixed.md:13239 (cited there at a stale path; live path is SyxxCLI/Commands/SyxxWitness.swift:53-57). D086 covers the --gen-keypair half.
- **Fix(es):**          Replace --key <b64> with --key-file (owner-only enforced) and/or Keychain; keep --key only behind --insecure-key-arg with a warning; create dir 0700 and file 0600 at creation, not chmod-after-write. Windows twin.

**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Added --key-file <path> (owner-only enforced); --key/-k gated behind --insecure-key-arg with warning; ~/.syxx created with 0700; key file written with 0600 atomically (temp file + move).

- **[B190]**               Trial anchor accepts an unauthenticated server body as authoritative and blocks on a semaphore
- **Criticality:**      Low-Medium
- **Location:**         LicenseManager.swift:161-188,209-235,237-239
- **Issue:**            syncTrialAnchorWithServer accepts firstLaunch/serverTime from an unsigned, unpinned body; serverLaunch overrides the locally-signed anchor unconditionally; a serverTime >300s ahead returns Date.distantPast, rendered as "🔒 Locked (Clock regression detected)". The local anchor's HMAC is keyed with a fingerprint derived from platform UUID + hardcoded salt — reproducible with local access, so a checksum not a MAC. Uses DispatchSemaphore.wait (1.5s) inside a sync function reached from async code.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** D001 (DELTAS.md:1822), D002, D009.
- **Fix(es):**          Require a server signature before a remote anchor overrides local; unverifiable → keep local, never distantPast; never render a definite clock-regression lock from unauthenticated time; make the call async.

**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Made syncTrialAnchorWithServer async (removed DispatchSemaphore); getFirstLaunchDate no longer returns Date.distantPast from unauthenticated serverTime; callers cannot be locked out by a forged server response.

- **[B191]**           Windows Glutton Collapses `Invalidated` into `Inconclusive` for a Structurally
                        Invalid / Errored Leak Payload — "ran, corrupt" Loses Its Hard Gate
- **Criticality:**      High. Direct violation of the engine's CRITICAL DISTINCTION
                        (`MatrixDeterminativeEngine.cs:45-51`): `Invalidated` is NOT usable and
                        hard-gates; `Inconclusive` IS usable and does not. Same evidence, different
                        verdict across platforms.
- **Location:**         `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/Synthesis/Synthesizer.cs:658-676` (esp. `:672-674`);
                        macOS twin `macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/Synthesizer.swift:471-483`
                        (esp. `:481-482`).
- **Issue:**            `LeakVerdict` has five cases on both platforms (`LeakDetector.cs:36-48`,
                        `LeakDetector.swift:21-32`). Swift maps them individually:
                        `.inconclusive → (signature == nil ? .invalidated : .inconclusive)`,
                        `.invalid → .invalidated`, `.error → .invalidated`. The C# switch handles
                        only `Confirmed` and `NotDetected` explicitly and sends `Inconclusive`,
                        `Invalid` AND `Error` through one `default:` arm that applies the
                        signature test to all three. So a leak result that is *structurally
                        impossible* (`Invalid`) or that *failed to execute* (`Error`) but that
                        happens to carry a signature is reported as **Inconclusive** on Windows and
                        **Invalidated** on macOS. `EvidenceUsability.FromSectionStatus`
                        (`MatrixDeterminativeEngine.cs:182-191`) then makes it `Inconclusive`
                        (`IsUsable() == true`, `:150-155`), so Glutton stops hard-gating
                        `dnsPrivacyLeak` / `transportDowngrade` (`SyxxMatrixIntegration.cs:320`,
                        `:278`) on Windows while it still gates on macOS. Corrupt data is being
                        treated as valid-but-unresolved — a `default:` arm silently absorbing two
                        states with different honesty semantics.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B043 (`DELTAS.md:5464`) quotes this same `default:` line but its Issue and
                        Fixes address only the early-`return`/ECS drop; the `Invalid`/`Error`
                        mis-mapping is not covered by B043 or any other open delta (greps of
                        DELTAS.md / DELTAS-fixed.md for `LeakVerdict`, `Invalidated`, `Glutton`).
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Enumerate all five verdicts explicitly, mirroring Swift:
                        `Invalid` and `Error` → `Invalidated` unconditionally; keep the signature
                        test only on `Inconclusive`. [Fix 2] Delete the `default:` arm so a new
                        `LeakVerdict` case is a compile error (CS8509 with
                        `<EnforceExhaustiveSwitch>` / analyzer) instead of a silent downgrade.
                        [Fix 3] Test: `Leak = { Verdict = Invalid, Signature = "sig" }` on both
                        platforms → Glutton section `Invalidated` and `dnsPrivacyLeak` suppressed
                        with reason `invalidatedRequiredSection`. Fails on Windows today.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/Synthesis/Synthesizer.cs:672-674
                        default:
                            return new SectionEvidence(leak.Signature is null ? SectionStatus.Invalidated : SectionStatus.Inconclusive,
                                Array.Empty<EvidenceItem>(), Array.Empty<Advisory>(), 0.0, 0, GluttonDatapointCatalog);

                        // macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/Synthesizer.swift:480-482
                        case .inconclusive:  subs.append(l.signature == nil ? .invalidated : .inconclusive)
                        case .invalid:       subs.append(.invalidated)
                        case .error:         subs.append(.invalidated)

                        ]


**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** All five LeakResult cases enumerated explicitly: Invalid/Error → Invalidated unconditionally; only Inconclusive applies the signature test.
---

- **[B192]**           Windows Glutton Still Drops the ECS Leak Signal — Every Leak Branch `return`s
                        Before the ECS Datapoint Is Read (re-confirmation of B043 at current lines)
- **Criticality:**      High. A measured Positive privacy finding is structurally invisible on
                        Windows whenever a leak result exists (the normal flow), and the section
                        reads `Negative` (clean support) where macOS reads `Positive`.
- **Location:**         `Synthesizer.cs:658-676` (all four exits `return`), dead code at
                        `:679-698` (`ecs` block) and `:700-701` (final combine);
                        macOS twin `Synthesizer.swift:471-500` (append + fall-through + `combine`).
- **Issue:**            Unchanged since B043 was filed and re-confirmed (2026-07-11); only the line
                        numbers moved (B043 cites `:566-604`). With `Leak.Verdict == NotDetected`
                        and `Ecs.Status == Forwarded`, macOS yields Glutton `Positive` plus an
                        `EcsSubnetLeak` evidence item; Windows yields Glutton `Negative` with no ECS
                        evidence and completeness pinned at `1.0 / GluttonDatapointCatalog`
                        (`:670-671`), i.e. a *measured* privacy exposure is deleted and the section
                        contributes clean support instead.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B043 (`DELTAS.md:5464`, OPEN, "Critical", re-confirmed 2026-07-11). This
                        entry re-confirms it at the current line numbers; no new delta needed.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] As B043 Fix 1 — accumulate into `subs`/`ev`, never `return`, then
                        `CombineWithEvidence` once at the end (this also fixes W1-01's shape).
                        [Fix 2] Parity test asserting both adapters produce identical
                        `SectionEvidence` for `{Leak: NotDetected, Ecs: Forwarded}`.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Synthesizer.cs:669-671 — a clean leak reading returns immediately;
                        // the ECS block at :679 can never run when leak != null.
                        case LeakVerdict.NotDetected:
                            return new SectionEvidence(SectionStatus.Negative, Array.Empty<EvidenceItem>(), Array.Empty<Advisory>(),
                                1.0 / GluttonDatapointCatalog, 1, GluttonDatapointCatalog);

                        ]


**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Same fix as B043: Glutton no longer returns early in leak branches, so ECS datapoint is always consumed.
---

- **[B193]**           Resolver-Latency Mapping Diverges Both Ways: `Unreachable` Hard-Gates Prophet
                        on Windows, `Slow` Reads as Clean Support
- **Criticality:**      High. `Unreachable` → `Invalidated` makes the whole Prophet section
                        `Invalidated` (`Combine`, `Synthesizer.cs:103`), which hard-gates `clean`,
                        `dnsSpoofing` and `dnsInterception` (required sections `prophet` at
                        `SyxxMatrixIntegration.cs:224, :250, :292`) and forces `Inconclusive`
                        where macOS can still return `Clean` or `ThreatDetected`. In the other
                        direction `Slow` supplies a `Negative` (clean) datapoint on Windows where
                        macOS records a quality knock.
- **Location:**         `Synthesizer.cs:175-191`; macOS twin `Synthesizer.swift:127-133`.
- **Issue:**            `ResolverLatencyStatus` is `{ Fast, Moderate, Slow, Degraded, Unreachable,
                        Untested }` (`ResolverLatencyProbe.cs:10`). The C# switch names only
                        `Degraded`, `Unreachable`, `Untested`; `Fast`, `Moderate` **and `Slow`**
                        fall into `default: subs.Add(SectionStatus.Negative)`. Swift maps
                        `.slow, .degraded, .unreachable → .degraded` and `.fast, .moderate →
                        .negative`. Two divergences from one `default:` arm: a slow resolver is
                        counted as evidence of cleanliness, and an unreachable one is escalated
                        from "run quality is poor" to "this section's premise failed", which the
                        engine treats as untrustworthy input. Swift's own comment
                        (`Synthesizer.swift:126`) states latency "never argues a threat, it just
                        reduces confidence" — `Invalidated` is a stronger claim than that contract.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** E016 (`DELTAS.md:5789`, OPEN) filed exactly this divergence. Still present;
                        line numbers have moved from `:5798`'s citation to `Synthesizer.cs:175-191`.
                        E016 predates the matrix cutover and does not state the new consequence:
                        under the matrix engine `Invalidated` on `prophet` is a *hard gate*, not a
                        weight, so the impact is now verdict-changing rather than confidence-shading.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Decide the mapping once, on both platforms, and write it as an
                        exhaustive switch with no `default:`: `Slow/Degraded/Unreachable → Degraded`
                        (adopt the macOS contract) or, if `Unreachable` should gate, change Swift
                        too and record the decision. [Fix 2] Add a shared fixture asserting the
                        section status for each of the six latency states on both platforms.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/Synthesis/Synthesizer.cs:177-190
                        switch (l.Status)
                        {
                            case ResolverLatencyStatus.Degraded:    subs.Add(SectionStatus.Degraded); break;
                            case ResolverLatencyStatus.Unreachable: subs.Add(SectionStatus.Invalidated); break;   // macOS: .degraded
                            case ResolverLatencyStatus.Untested:    break;
                            default:                                subs.Add(SectionStatus.Negative); break;      // swallows Slow
                        }

                        // macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/Synthesizer.swift:128-132
                        switch lat.status {
                        case .slow, .degraded, .unreachable: subs.append(.degraded)
                        case .fast, .moderate:               subs.append(.negative)
                        case .untested:                      break
                        }

                        ]


**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Latency mapping now matches macOS: Slow/Degraded/Unreachable → Degraded; Fast/Moderate → Negative. No default arm.
---

- **[B194]**           `MergedDatapoint.IsConflicting` Uses the Wrong Quantifier — a Genuine
                        Cross-Vantage Answer-Set Disagreement Is Suppressed on Windows
- **Criticality:**      High. The Windows port of the B040 set-vs-scalar fix inverted the loop:
                        Swift flags a conflict when **any** later value's section-set is disjoint
                        from the first; C# returns "no conflict" as soon as **any** later set
                        overlaps. A real Prophet≠Mirror divergence is rendered as an ordinary FACTS
                        line instead of `⚠ … DISCREPANCY`.
- **Location:**         `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/Synthesis/Report.cs:21-35` (esp. `:29-33`);
                        macOS twin `macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/Report.swift:39-52`
                        (esp. `:45-51`).
- **Issue:**            Worked example (Mirror returns two addresses, Prophet returns a third,
                        wholly different one — the exact multi-record case B040's fix was written
                        for): values are `A1→[mirror]`, `A2→[mirror]`, `A3→[prophet]`;
                        `allSets = [{m},{m},{p}]`. Swift: `i=1` `{m}` not disjoint from `{m}`;
                        `i=2` `{p}` **is** disjoint → `true` (conflict, correct — the two sections'
                        answer sets share nothing). C#: `i=1` `{m}.Overlaps({m})` → `return false`
                        (no conflict). The two-value case coincidentally agrees, which is why this
                        survives casual testing. Downstream, `SynthesisReport.Conflicts`
                        (`Report.cs:107`) and the `⚠ … DISCREPANCY` line (`:157`) are both silenced
                        — a cross-discipline answer disagreement, the single strongest raw-fact
                        signal the report has, reads clean on Windows.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B040 (`DELTAS.md:5152`, OPEN, "Critical") is the parent — it specified the
                        set-valued treatment. The macOS implementation matches B040 Fix 2; the
                        Windows implementation of the same fix is logically inverted. Not covered
                        by any existing delta text.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Mirror Swift exactly: `for (int i = 1; i < allSets.Count; i++) if
                        (!allSets[i].Overlaps(first)) return true; return false;`. [Fix 2] Golden
                        vector on both platforms: `{mirror:[A1,A2], prophet:[A3]} →
                        IsConflicting == true`; `{mirror:[A1,A2], prophet:[A1,A3]} → false`.
                        Windows fails case 1 today.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/Synthesis/Report.cs:29-33
                        var allSets = Values.Select(v => new HashSet<Section>(v.Sections)).ToList();
                        var first = allSets[0];
                        for (int i = 1; i < allSets.Count; i++)
                            if (allSets[i].Overlaps(first)) return false;   // ANY overlap => not conflicting
                        return true;

                        // macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/Report.swift:48-51
                        for i in 1..<allSets.count {
                            if allSets[i].isDisjoint(with: first) { return true }   // ANY disjoint => conflicting
                        }
                        return false

                        ]


**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** IsConflicting quantifier corrected: returns true when ANY section-set is disjoint (matching macOS isDisjoint logic).
---

- **[B195]**           Three Prophet/Mirror Datapoints Discard Sub-Statuses macOS Records — an
                        Inconclusive Reading Contributes Nothing and the Section Can Combine to
                        `Negative`
- **Criticality:**      Medium-High. "Missing data is not clean data": on Windows an inconclusive
                        stability or dual-stack reading is dropped from `subs` entirely, so
                        `Combine` (`Synthesizer.cs:100-107`) can return `Negative` (a real clean
                        datapoint feeding `EvidenceUsability.Failed`) where macOS returns
                        `Inconclusive`.
- **Location:**         `Synthesizer.cs:207-209` (stability `Inconclusive` + `Untested → break`),
                        `:260-262` (denial `NotADenial` + `Untested → break`), `:280-282`
                        (dualStack `Inconclusive` + `Untested → break`); macOS twins
                        `Synthesizer.swift:146` (`.inconclusive → .inconclusive`), `:184`
                        (`.notADenial → .negative`), `:200` (`.inconclusive → .inconclusive`).
- **Issue:**            All three C# switches merge a *ran-but-unresolved* (or, for denial, a
                        *ran-and-clean*) state into the same `break` used for *never ran*. The
                        untested/inconclusive distinction the engine's header comment calls
                        structural (`MatrixDeterminativeEngine.cs:45-51`) is erased at the adapter
                        boundary, before the engine can see it. Direction differs per case —
                        stability/dualStack lose an abstention (reads cleaner), denial loses a
                        genuine negative (reads less complete) — but both are the same defect:
                        a state that exists on one platform is unrepresentable on the other.
                        Note the enums do carry the cases: `AnswerStabilityStatus.Inconclusive`
                        (`AnswerStabilityProbe.cs:8`), `DenialStatus.NotADenial`
                        (`DenialOfExistenceProbe.cs:8`), `DualStackStatus.Inconclusive`
                        (`DualStackProbe.cs:10`).
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** No open delta covers these three (greps of DELTAS.md / DELTAS-fixed.md for
                        `AnswerStability`, `DualStack`, `NotADenial`). Same defect class as E016
                        (W1-03) and as the fixed B136/Mirror empty-set case (`Synthesizer.cs:372-381`).
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Separate the `Inconclusive` and `Untested` labels in each switch and
                        add `NotADenial → Negative`, matching Swift case-for-case. [Fix 2] Drop the
                        `default:` arms in these three switches so a future enum case cannot be
                        absorbed silently. [Fix 3] Table-driven parity test: for each probe status
                        on both platforms, assert the resulting section status.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Synthesizer.cs:207-209 — "ran, ambiguous" and "never ran" share one arm
                        case AnswerStabilityStatus.Inconclusive:
                        case AnswerStabilityStatus.Untested:
                            break;

                        // Synthesizer.swift:146-147 — distinct
                        case .inconclusive:      subs.append(.inconclusive)
                        case .untested:          break

                        ]


**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Stability Inconclusive → .inconclusive; denial NotADenial → .negative; dual-stack Inconclusive → .inconclusive. No default arms.
---

- **[B196]**           `SynthesisReport.RenderText` Renders Gate-Only Engine Metrics (with
                        Culture-Sensitive Formatting), Uses PascalCase Wire Labels, and Omits
                        Advisories — Three Divergences from the macOS Report
- **Criticality:**      Medium. `MATRIX-MIGRATION-PLAN.md:260` binds the Windows port: "weights/
                        thresholds NEVER surface to users … grep the Windows surfaces for any
                        rendering of fit/coverage/margin beyond what macOS already shows", echoed
                        in `SyxxMatrixIntegration.cs:15-18`. These are uncalibrated expert
                        constants (Phase 6 is blocked on a labeled corpus), so printing them as
                        percentages presents unearned precision as measurement.
- **Location:**         `Report.cs:136` (`fit {top.Fit:P0}, coverage {top.Coverage:P0}`),
                        `:176-181` (per-diagnosis `d.Explanation`, which embeds fit/coverage/
                        margin/contradiction — see `MatrixDeterminativeEngine.cs:1144-1147`),
                        `:142` (`[{s.Reason}]`), `:136` (`({top.Confidence})`), `:165-171`
                        (evidence + sections, then nothing); macOS twin `Report.swift:129-182`,
                        which renders no per-diagnosis explanation, uses `reason.rawValue` /
                        `confidence.rawValue`, and DOES render `EXPOSURE FINDINGS` at `:176-180`.
- **Issue:**            Three distinct problems in one method. (1) Gate-only numbers reach the
                        user, and `:P0` is a **CurrentCulture** format — on a `de-DE` or `fr-FR`
                        host the same evidence prints a different string (NBSP separator, differing
                        symbol placement), so the report is not reproducible across locales.
                        (2) `{s.Reason}` and `{top.Confidence}` interpolate C# enum member names
                        (`MissingRequiredSection`, `Probable`) where macOS prints the Codable
                        rawValues (`missingRequiredSection`, `probable`) — the same field has two
                        spellings across platforms. (3) `Conclusion.Advisories` is never rendered,
                        so the open-resolver exposure finding that Portal deliberately routes there
                        (`Synthesizer.cs:622-632`) cannot reach the text report at all.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** B041 (`DELTAS.md:5281`, OPEN) filed the dropped-advisories omission against
                        BOTH platforms; macOS has since fixed it (`Report.swift:176-180`) and
                        Windows has not, so B041 is now a one-sided parity gap. B042
                        (`DELTAS.md:5374`) is adjacent but concerns the removed DST
                        `conflict`/`uncertainty` fields. Nothing covers the gate-only rendering or
                        the culture-sensitive format.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Delete the `fit`/`coverage` clause at `:136` and the per-diagnosis
                        `Explanation` block at `:176-181`; if any number must ship, gate it behind
                        an explicit debug/diagnostic flag, never the user report. [Fix 2] Use
                        `RawValue()` extensions for `Confidence`; add a `RawValue()` for
                        `SuppressedDiagnosis.SuppressionReason` (the converter at `:641-649`
                        already holds the strings) and use it. [Fix 3] Port the `EXPOSURE FINDINGS`
                        block from `Report.swift:176-180`. [Fix 4] If a percentage is ever
                        rendered, pass `CultureInfo.InvariantCulture` explicitly.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/Synthesis/Report.cs:136
                        o.Add($"  Top diagnosis: {top.DisplayName} ({top.Confidence}) — fit {top.Fit:P0}, coverage {top.Coverage:P0}");
                        // Report.cs:142
                        o.Add($"    - {s.DisplayName} [{s.Reason}]");

                        // macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/Report.swift:148-149 — rawValue, no metrics
                        out.append("Unverified signals: " + det.suppressed
                            .map { "\($0.displayName) (\($0.reason.rawValue))" }.joined(separator: ", "))

                        ]


**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Removed gate-only metrics (fit/coverage) from user report; used rawValue for enums; added EXPOSURE FINDINGS block for advisories.
---

- **[B197]**           `EvidenceId` Has No JSON Converter — Windows Serializes It as an Integer
                        Ordinal Where macOS Emits the camelCase String
- **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** Added EvidenceIdJsonConverter emitting exact Swift rawValues; added EvidenceIdExtensions.RawValue() for canonical byte construction; updated CanonicalBytes() to use rawValues instead of PascalCase member names.
- **Criticality:**      Medium (latent: `Conclusion` is not serialized on a production Windows path
                        today, but every property carries a `JsonPropertyName` and the file is
                        declared the wire twin of `Conclusion.swift`).
- **Location:**         `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/Synthesis/Conclusion.cs:73-112` (`enum EvidenceId`,
                        no `[JsonConverter]`), consumed at `:51`
                        (`[property: JsonPropertyName("evidenceId")] EvidenceId EvidenceId`);
                        macOS twin `Conclusion.swift:98-136` (`enum EvidenceId: String, Codable`).
- **Issue:**            Every other enum in the file has an explicit converter — `Section`
                        (`:115-142`), `SectionStatus` (`:146-179`), `Severity` (`:194-221`),
                        `AdvisoryId` (`:232-249`) — and the matrix engine file goes out of its way
                        to state that enums serialize "via EXPLICIT converters — never enum-name
                        reflection — so a rename can never silently fork the JSON schema"
                        (`MatrixDeterminativeEngine.cs:22-23`). `EvidenceId` is the one enum that
                        was missed, so `System.Text.Json` falls back to the numeric ordinal: the
                        same evidence item serializes as `{"section":"prophet","evidenceId":18,…}`
                        on Windows and `{"section":"prophet","evidenceId":"authoritativeDiverges",…}`
                        on macOS. Worse, the ordinal is **positional** — inserting a case anywhere
                        but the end silently re-labels every previously written record.
                        Related: `ValidatedDecisiveActs.CanonicalBytes()` (`:305-310`) builds
                        signature material from `a.ToString()`, i.e. the PascalCase member names,
                        which are not the wire rawValues used anywhere else.
