- **[B206]**           **FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code), already resolved.** macOS `SectionEvidence` (Synthesizer.swift:14) and `SectionStatusEntry` (Conclusion.swift:162,175) already apply the `[0,1]` clamp. C# twin still needs the fix.
                        `SectionEvidence` / `SectionStatusEntry` Drop the `[0,1]` Completeness Clamp
                        That Swift Applies at Construction
- **Criticality:**      Low (defence-in-depth: no current caller passes an out-of-range value; the
                        matrix path re-clamps at `SynthesisEngine.cs:190`).
- **Location:**         `Synthesizer.cs:10` (positional record, no clamp),
                        `Conclusion.cs:181-190` (ditto); macOS twins `Synthesizer.swift:12-17`
                        (`completeness = max(0.0, min(1.0, completeness))`) and
                        `Conclusion.swift:161-165` (same, plus a `decodeIfPresent … ?? 1.0`
                        decoder at `:169-174`).
- **Issue:**            An out-of-range completeness would survive into `SectionStatusEntry`,
                        into the serialized report, and into any UI that renders it as a
                        percentage, while the matrix quality input would be clamped — two
                        different numbers describing the same measurement. Swift also clamps on
                        decode; the C# record does not, so a hand-edited or foreign JSON is
                        accepted verbatim.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** None found.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Add an `init`-time clamp on both records (an `init` accessor or a
                        factory), matching Swift.
