- **[C071]**               NXDOMAIN-Hijack Canary Only Detects Hijacks That Share A Single IP — CNAME And
**FIXED 2026-07-30 (qwen-code).** NXDOMAIN canary now detects shared CNAME targets and escalates to positive when both random names return any answer (decisive: 96-bit entropy names cannot legitimately resolve).
                        Per-Name Redirects Read As "Atypical"
- **Criticality:**      Moderate (detection gap in the canary the whole Prophet section leans on).
- **Location:**         macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/DNSCanaries.swift:60-78
- **Issue:**            The positive branch requires two unrelated random names to resolve to the SAME
                        A record (:60-63). Two common real-world hijack shapes miss it:
                        (a) the redirector answers with a CNAME to a search/ad page and no A record —
                        `records.filter { $0.type == 1 }` is empty for both names, so `shared` is
                        empty; and with `requestedType == 1` a CNAME-only answer is `.noData`, not a
                        failure, so the result is `.inconclusive`;
                        (b) the redirector round-robins or hashes the name to different IPs — again no
                        shared member.
                        Both land in the `.inconclusive` "atypical, non-matching answers" branch
                        (:72-78) — a weak signal — when the observation "two random nonexistent names
                        both returned answers" is by itself decisive: a name with 96 bits of entropy
                        cannot exist.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** none found.
- **Fix(es):**          [Fix 1] Escalate to `.positive` when BOTH canaries return any answer (A, AAAA
                        or CNAME) regardless of overlap — a random 24-hex-digit label under `.com`
                        cannot legitimately resolve. Keep the shared-IP case as the strongest wording.
                        [Fix 2] Compare CNAME targets as well as A rdata for the "shared destination"
                        test.
                        [Fix 3] Windows twin.
- **Code Sample:**      [

                        // DNSCanaries.swift:60-63
                        let ips1 = Set(r1.records.filter { $0.type == 1 }.map { $0.rdata })
                        let ips2 = Set(r2.records.filter { $0.type == 1 }.map { $0.rdata })
                        let shared = ips1.intersection(ips2)
                        if let ip = shared.first, !ip.isEmpty {

                        ]

