**[E148]** W3-25 — UpdatePanel hard-codes a fourth, divergent severity palette
**Criticality:** MEDIUM (the exact drift `SeverityBrush` exists to prevent)
**Location:** `Windows/dotNET/SyxxUI/Views/UpdatePanel.xaml.cs:141-145` vs `Styles/IoDesignSystem.xaml:38-42`
**Issue:** `IoSuccessGreen => #22C55E` (design system: `#2DD4BF`), `IoWarningAmber => #ED9A14`
(`#F7931E`), `IoCriticalRed => #EF4444` (`#FF4D6D`), `IoTextSecondary => #A99FC8` (`#C9C1E6`). Four
colours, none matching, all named as if they were the tokens. `Presentation/SeverityBrush.cs:8-15`
states the rule this breaks verbatim: "two mappings eventually disagree, and a verdict that grades a
different colour on each surface is exactly the false-confidence this app refuses."
**Cross-ref. Delta:** SeverityBrush single-source rule.
**Fix(es):** Delete `:141-145` and resolve from `Application.Current.Resources["IoSuccessGreenColor"]`
etc. (the pattern `DashboardView.xaml.cs:30-34` already uses).
**Code Sample:**
```csharp
// UpdatePanel.xaml.cs:142-145
private static Color IoTextSecondary => Color.FromArgb(0xFF, 0xA9, 0x9F, 0xC8);
private static Color IoSuccessGreen  => Color.FromArgb(0xFF, 0x22, 0xC5, 0x5E);
private static Color IoWarningAmber  => Color.FromArgb(0xFF, 0xED, 0x9A, 0x14);
private static Color IoCriticalRed   => Color.FromArgb(0xFF, 0xEF, 0x44, 0x44);
```

