## Notes on what was checked and found sound

- `Presentation/SeverityBrush.cs` — correct single-source mapping; `Unknown` → neutral, never green.
- `Views/WitnessView.xaml.cs:125-200` — the verify verdict is genuinely tri-state+ and every
  `SnapshotVerification` member (`Syxx.Kit/WitnessSigner.cs:274-293`) is mapped, with tamper/forgery
  red and freshness/trust amber. Only `Valid` is green. No static "signature valid" element exists.
- `Views/GluttonView.xaml.cs:126-138` — the D004 attestation gate is real and correctly blocks a
  confident verdict on an unsigned/untrusted result.
- `Views/PortalView.xaml.cs:79-114` — traceroute failure states are honest ("This is not evidence of
  a clean path."); `IsSafeTraceTarget` (`:159-164`) properly rejects flag-like and whitespace input.
- `Views/RemoteContentPanel.xaml.cs:88-156` — two-layer fallback, embedded-resource name resolved
  rather than assumed, per-status diagnosis kept in step with the macOS twin.
- `Views/LicenseView.xaml.cs:65-91` — activation correctly distinguishes Invalid from
  Timeout/NetworkError (D043) and verified-but-unsaved (D042).
- `Services/DimensionService.cs` — `IsVersionInRange` bounds check is correct in both directions;
  corrupt settings fail to empty rather than blocking startup.
- `Services/TrayIconService.cs` — icon/tooltip come from the Kit's `TrayIndicators`, grey before any
  scan; old `HICON` is destroyed on update; `Dispose` removes the icon.
- All other `Syxx.Kit` types referenced by `SyxxUI` were verified present with matching members:
  `ComparisonResult`, `ResolverQueryOutcome`, `WarpResult`, `NetworkStateReport`, `LeakTestResult`,
  `WitnessSnapshot`, `SnapshotVerification`, `DeterminativeResult`, `ReportCard`/`ReportCards`,
  `VerdictActions`, `PlainExplanations`, `FirstRunResult.Inputs`, `WitnessTestifyPlanner.Outcome`,
  `MonitorTick`/`MonitorState`, `ExfilAnalyzer.Assess`, `ProbeRunner.RunAsync<T> where T : class`.
  The only dangling references are W3-01, W3-02, W3-03.

- **[F037]**               `testAllUntestedSections_isUntestedWithGatedDiagnoses` Is a Softened Test: Name and Comments Say "Untested", the Fixture Is `.inconclusive`, and the Assertion Was Widened to Accept Any Suppression Reason
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         macOS/Tests/SyxxKitTests/MatrixDeterminativeEngineTests.swift:357-384
                        (subject: macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/Synthesis/MatrixDeterminativeEngine.swift:102-107,795-814)
- **Issue:**            Every textual claim in this test is false against its own fixture.
                        • The name says `AllUntestedSections` and `isUntested`; the header comment
                          (`:359-360`) says "all sections untested → `.inconclusive`, required-section
                          diagnoses **gated**"; the inline comment (`:372`) says "untested required
                          sections gate it"; (`:379`) "all untested → gated".
                        • The fixture (`:363-370`) builds six `SectionResult(status: .inconclusive)`.
                          There is a real, distinct `EvidenceUsability.untested` case
                          (MatrixDeterminativeEngine.swift:95) which the file never uses.
                        • `.inconclusive.isUsable == true` (:104) so the required-section gate loop
                          (:809) does **not** fire. `sectionByID` is consulted *only* by that gate
                          (`rawScore` :731-814 reads section status nowhere else), so section status
                          cannot attenuate anything. The suppression that actually occurs is
                          `belowTier` — the identical outcome the same fixture would produce with
                          `allSectionsPassed()`. The gating path the test is named for is never
                          reached.
                        • The comment at `:380-381` — "the engine may suppress as belowTier (quality
                          attenuation from untested sections) or missingRequiredSection. Both are
                          correct gating outcomes." — is both a widening rationalisation and
                          technically wrong: no quality attenuation from section status exists in the
                          engine. The assertion (`:382`) was consequently reduced to
                          `suppressed.contains { $0.id.rawValue == "dnsSpoofing" }` with **no reason
                          pinned**, unlike its five sibling tests in the same file that all pin the
                          exact reason (`:438` `.missingRequiredSection`, `:456`
                          `.invalidatedRequiredSection`, `:485` `.hardExcluded`, `:50`/`:75`/`:106`/`:148`
                          `.belowTier`). The signature of hitting red and editing the test.
                        Net effect: a reviewer reading the suite believes never-ran sections are
                        proven to hard-gate. They are not proven here. The test would stay green if
                        `.untested` were reclassified `isUsable == true` (honesty spine rule 2
                        inverted), and it would stay green if `missingRequiredSection` gating were
                        deleted outright.
- **Mitigating:**       The `.untested` contract IS genuinely covered elsewhere, by the untracked
                        `macOS/Tests/SyxxKitTests/MatrixUntestedStateTests.swift` (`:24-28` uses
                        `status: .untested`; `:43-46`, `:83-87` pin positive states). That file is a
                        model of the discipline this one lacks — and it is `??` untracked in git
                        (`git status --porcelain`), while the softened file is staged `A`. If the
                        untracked file is not committed, the `.untested` branch
                        (MatrixDeterminativeEngine.swift:611-616) has **zero** coverage.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** C062 is cited in the widening comment (`:380`) as the justification; C062 is
                        about overhang admission, not about section-status gating — the delta is
                        being invoked to license the widening.
- **Fix(es):**          Rename to `testAllInconclusiveSections_…` and pin `.reason == .belowTier`
                        (the outcome that actually occurs); then add the missing test with
                        `status: .untested` pinning `.reason == .missingRequiredSection`. Commit
                        `MatrixUntestedStateTests.swift`. Delete the `:380-381` comment.
- **Code Sample:**      [
                        // :361-383 — name/fixture/assertion all disagree
                        func testAllUntestedSections_isUntestedWithGatedDiagnoses() throws {
                            sections: [ SectionResult(id: SectionID("prophet"), status: .inconclusive), … ]
                            //                                                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ not .untested
                            // C062: the engine may suppress as belowTier (quality attenuation from untested
                            // sections) or missingRequiredSection. Both are correct gating outcomes.
                            XCTAssertTrue(result.suppressed.contains { $0.id.rawValue == "dnsSpoofing" })
                            //                                        ^ no `&& $0.reason == …` — cannot detect which gate regressed
                        }
                        ]

---

- **[F038]**               A Malformed Signing Key That Successfully SIGNS Is Reported as a SKIP, Not a Failure — the `XCTSkip` Guard Fires on Exactly the Catastrophic Outcome and Blames the Network
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         macOS/Tests/SyxxAppTests/AppModelWitnessTests.swift:268-288 (guard at :279-281)
                        (subject: macOS/Sources/SyxxApp/AppModel.swift `runWitness()`;
                        macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/…/WitnessSigner / WitnessKeyManager)
- **Issue:**            The test installs a deliberately invalid key (`saveKey("not-a-valid-base64-key!!!")`,
                        :272) so `WitnessSigner.createSignedSnapshot` MUST throw, then runs the real
                        pipeline and does:
                          `guard case .failed(let msg) = model.witnessTestimonyState else { throw XCTSkip(…) }`
                        `waitUntilTerminal` (:68-82) only returns on `.signed` or `.failed` (or on a
                        timeout it has already `XCTFail`ed). Therefore the `else` branch is reached in
                        exactly one non-already-failing case: **the state is `.signed`** — a garbage,
                        cryptographically-invalid key produced a *signed witness snapshot*. That is
                        the single worst outcome this file could observe (a forgeable attestation
                        presented as valid evidence), and the test converts it into a green skip.
                        The skip's own text blames the network — "if this environment has no network
                        the vantage-comparison leg may fail first with a different (still non-generic)
                        message" — but that scenario yields `.failed` and so takes the `guard`'s
                        *success* path, where the `hasPrefix` assertion at :283 would legitimately go
                        red. The stated rationale describes a case the guard cannot reach. The guard
                        cannot distinguish "environment unavailable" from "the signing guard is
                        broken"; there is no network probe, no reachability check, nothing.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** D097 Fix 2 (cited at :283, :286). The delta's regression guard is the thing
                        being skipped.
- **Fix(es):**          Replace the guard with an explicit fail:
                        `if case .signed = state { XCTFail("a malformed key produced a SIGNED snapshot — forgeable attestation") ; return }`
                        and only skip on a *proven* connectivity failure (e.g. a pre-flight resolver
                        reachability check whose failure is asserted separately).

---

- **[F039]**               The D096 and D097 Success-Path Regression Guards `XCTSkip` Whenever the Feature Is Broken — a Total Witness Failure Reads Green
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         macOS/Tests/SyxxAppTests/AppModelWitnessTests.swift:206-208 (D096),
                        :244-246 (D097)
- **Issue:**            Both tests do a real `witnessTestify()` / `run()` and then
                        `guard case .signed = model.witnessTestimonyState else { throw XCTSkip("… likely
                        no network egress here") }`. The guard's *only* input is the product's own
                        terminal state, so "the machine has no route to 1.1.1.1" and "`runWitness()`
                        now always lands `.failed`" are indistinguishable to it — and both are
                        reported as SKIPPED, which `swift test` exits 0 on. The two tests carry the
                        entire regression coverage for D096 (Free must null the snapshot / must not
                        resurrect the prior `.valid` verdict) and D097 (Run-All routes through real
                        published state), and both self-disable precisely when the Witness pipeline
                        stops working. The CI leg (`.github/workflows/ci.yml:114-116`,
                        `swift test --package-path macOS` on `macos-14`) has network egress, so a
                        genuine product regression here is silently downgraded to a skip in the only
                        place it would be caught.
- **Mitigating:**       The file is otherwise unusually honest — `:154-168` documents that
                        `testRapidReTestify…` is probabilistic, and `:292-317` documents the D052 gap
                        rather than faking it. The defect is the skip *mechanism*, not intent.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** D096, D097.
- **Fix(es):**          Gate the skip on an independently-measured precondition (a reachability probe
                        run in `setUp`, whose failure is itself recorded), not on the subject's own
                        output. Absent that, `XCTFail` — an unreachable resolver on a CI runner is
                        itself a fact worth failing on.

---

- **[F040]**               `TrayGlyphTests` — the False-Clean Guard for the Most Glanceable Surface in the App — Is Quarantined Out of the Build AND Is RED Against Current Code
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         macOS/Tests/_quarantine/TrayGlyphTests.swift:25-26
                        (subject: macOS/Sources/SyxxApp/Views/Components/IoDesignSystem.swift:686-697,
                        colour constants at :39, :51, :52)
- **Issue:**            Two compounding problems.
                        (a) **Stale/red assertion.** The test pins
                            `XCTAssertEqual(Color.ioSeverity(.unknown), .ioTextSecondary, "unknown must be the neutral grey")`
                            The subject returns `.ioTextMuted` for `.unknown`
                            (IoDesignSystem.swift:694). These are different colours:
                            `ioTextSecondary = ioDynamic(light: 0x6a4fa0, dark: 0xc9c1e6)` (:51) vs
                            `ioTextMuted = ioDynamic(light: 0x9a8fc0, dark: 0x9a8fc0)` (:52). The
                            assertion is a live failure parked out of sight.
                        (b) **The whole file is unbuildable by design.** `macOS/Package.swift` declares
                            test targets `SyxxKitTests` and `SyxxAppTests` only; SwiftPM resolves those
                            to `Tests/SyxxKitTests` / `Tests/SyxxAppTests`. `macOS/Tests/_quarantine/`
                            is in no target, so its 19 files are never compiled, never run, and never
                            type-checked. Nothing in `macOS/`, `.github/`, or `scripts/` references
                            `_quarantine`. The genuinely valuable guards die with it, notably
                            `testNeverScannedIsUnknownNotClean` (:13-18 — nil conclusion must resolve
                            `.unknown`, never `.clean`) and `testOnlyCleanGetsTheSuccessGreen`
                            (:29-40 — the success green is reserved for exactly `.clean`). Their
                            subjects (`TrayIndicators.For`, SyxxKit/Synthesis/TrayIndicator.swift:42;
                            `TrayGlyph.syxxTemplateMark`, SyxxApp.swift:219; `Color.ioSeverity`) all
                            still exist and still ship — so the false-clean rule for the menu bar is
                            currently enforced by nothing.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** A6 (cited in the file header).
- **Fix(es):**          Move the three colour/state tests into `Tests/SyxxAppTests/`, correct the
                        `.ioTextSecondary` pin to `.ioTextMuted` (or fix the subject if secondary was
                        the intent — the *design* question must be answered, not the *test*), and
                        assert the honest property directly: `ioSeverity(.unknown) != ioSeverity(.clean)`
                        plus a non-green check, rather than pinning an exact palette constant.

