- **[F046]**               The Entire Windows Probe / Crypto / Resolver Test Suite (321 Assertions, 40+ Classes) Was Archived Out of the Build While Its Subjects Still Ship and the macOS Twin Suite Stays Active — CI's Windows Leg Is Green Over an Untested Core
- **Criticality:**      Critical
- **Location:**         archive/dst-tests-windows/SyxxKitTests.cs (4,667 lines, 321 `[Fact]`/`[Theory]`,
                        40+ test classes); no `.csproj` anywhere includes `archive/**`
                        (`find . -name '*.csproj'` — the directory is in no project);
                        .github/workflows/ci.yml:100-102 runs only
                        `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Tests/Syxx.Tests.csproj`
- **Issue:**            The archived file's own header (`:15-17`) states what it is: "These mirror the
                        offline-testable subset of the macOS SyxxKitTests XCTest suite (P3)." It is
                        the Windows twin of `macOS/Tests/SyxxKitTests/SyxxKitTests.swift` (129 active
                        `func test`s) plus the per-probe files. Its classes include
                        `IPClassifierTests`, `WitnessSignerTests`, `DNSCaptureTests`,
                        `DNSResolverTests`, `VantageComparerTests`, `TriStateVerdictTests`,
                        `DNSSECTests`, `ForensicsAnalyzerTests`, `TrustLedgerAndSentinelTests`,
                        `ProbeRunnerTests`, and thirteen per-probe classes (AnswerStability,
                        DualStack, CNAMEChain, DenialOfExistence, TTLAnomaly, SVCB, VersionBind,
                        EncryptedTransport, OpenResolver, ResolverLatency, StubEntropy,
                        WildcardSynthesis, ResponseSize).
                        The subjects all still ship: `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/IPClassifier.cs`,
                        `WitnessSigner.cs`, `VantageComparer.cs`, `ProbeRunner.cs`,
                        `AnswerStabilityProbe.cs`, `StubEntropyProbe.cs`, `DNSResolver.cs`, …
                        Active Windows coverage for them (grep across `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Tests`):
                          • `VantageComparer` — **no test file mentions it**
                          • `IPClassifier` — **none**
                          • `AnswerStabilityProbe`, `StubEntropyProbe` and every other probe except
                            ECS — **none**
                          • `DNSResolver` — mentioned only incidentally by
                            `TelemetryOutcomeHonestyTests.cs`
                          • `ProbeRunner` — only incidentally, by `TelemetryOutcomeHonestyTests.cs`
                            and `ECSProbeTests.cs`
                        The archive directory name ("dst-tests-windows") implies the retirement of the
                        DST synthesis engine, but this file tests almost nothing DST-related — IP
                        classification, Ed25519 signing, DNS parsing and the probe layer are
                        orthogonal to the synthesis rewrite and are the evidence-production layer the
                        whole product rests on.
                        Effect: `ci.yml:100-102` reports a green Windows leg for a reasoning core
                        whose evidence producers have no automated verification, while the identical
                        macOS code paths are covered by ~19 active Swift test files. This is
                        doctrine-6 (platform parity) inverted: parity is claimed in the CI header
                        ("Parity is a deliverable, not a hope", ci.yml:4-5) and the twin suite is
                        outside the build.
- **Cross-ref. Delta:** none found. Note `archive/dst-tests-windows/TrayIndicatorTests.cs` (9 tests)
                        and `ReportCardTests.cs`/`VerdictActionTests.cs` are archived too — the
                        Windows twins of the quarantined macOS false-clean guards, so BOTH platforms
                        lost the same guard layer in the same move.
- **Fix(es):**          Split `archive/dst-tests-windows/SyxxKitTests.cs`: return every class whose
                        subject still ships (all of the above) to `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Tests/`, and
                        keep archived only the classes whose subject was genuinely deleted. Run once
                        and accept whatever red comes out — a failing honesty test is data.

