## Notes on non-findings (checked, no defect filed)

- `MonitorScheduler` is `@MainActor` and its `onTick` is `@MainActor`-typed
  (SyxxKit/Trust/MonitorScheduler.swift:20, :34) — the `[weak self]` closure at
  AppModel.swift:171-190 is not a cross-actor mutation.
- `dashboardLog` is correctly capped at 200 (AppModel.swift:129-131) — the only bounded
  collection in the model; contrast M6-16.
- `SyxxSpeed`'s `.degraded` ranking is sound: `ResolverLatencyProbe` only emits
  `.degraded` with `successCount >= 1` (SyxxKit/ResolverLatencyProbe.swift:73, :81-84),
  so a zero-sample resolver cannot sort as 0 ms/fastest.
- `SyxxLan`'s `padding(toLength:)` truncation (Commands/SyxxLan.swift:29-31) cannot
  misprint an address: the source is `arp -an`, IPv4 only
  (SyxxKit/LAN/LANCartographer.swift:97), and the longest `addressKind` rawValue
  ("Private unicast") is exactly 15 characters.
- `witnessTestify` / `witnessFree` / `witnessVerify` (AppModel.swift:1503-1616) hold the
  cancellation and tri-state discipline they claim; no defect found.

## D079 remediation follow-up — 2026-07-29 (syxx-engineer)

**[D079] status: PARTIALLY REMEDIATED, install path still UNVERIFIED.**
Fixes 1-3 landed: `macOS/Package.swift:79-96` now embeds `__TEXT,__launchd_plist`
alongside `__info_plist`; `macOS/Sources/DeepSyxx/main.swift:259-268` claims the Mach
service with `NSXPCListener(machServiceName:)`; `macOS/bundle-app.sh:25-98` drops the
useless sidecar plist and hard-fails the build if the section is missing, unparseable,
or disagrees with the expected Label / MachServices / ProgramArguments.
Fix 4 (migrate to `SMAppService.daemon`) is NOT done. **`SMJobBless` itself has not been
exercised** — it needs an admin authorization prompt and writes to
`/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools`, neither of which was performed. The section is proven
present in the built and signed binary; the *installation* is not proven. D079 must stay
OPEN until someone runs `bundle-app.sh`, launches the app, and observes the helper appear
at `/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/com.milkandgin.deepsyxx`.

---

**[D116]**
**Criticality:** CRITICAL — Cage capture stays dead even after D079's install path is fixed
**Location:** `macOS/Sources/DeepSyxx/main.swift:26-34`; `macOS/Sources/SyxxApp/AppModel.swift:1132`
**Issue:** The privileged helper locates the `syxx` CLI it must exec via
`Bundle.main.bundlePath`. For a SMJobBless helper — a bare Mach-O executable launched by
launchd from `/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/com.milkandgin.deepsyxx`, with no `.app`
wrapper — `Bundle.main.bundlePath` is the *containing directory*, i.e.
`/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools` (verified empirically: a bare executable run from
`<dir>/prog` prints `<dir>`). The helper therefore looks for
`/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/Contents/Resources/syxx`, which never exists, and
`startCageCapture` replies `syxx binary not found at ...` for every request.
The app already knows the right path — `AppModel.swift:1132` computes
`Self.locateCageBinary()` — but never sends it; the compiler flags `bin` as
"defined but never used". `SyxxPrivilegedHelperProtocol.startCageCapture` has no path
parameter.
This is a *dependent* defect: it is masked today because D079 stops execution earlier, and
it becomes the visible symptom the moment D079's install path works.
**Honesty note (no regression):** the failure is reported honestly. The reply's error string
propagates to `AppModel.swift:1167` → `cageLaunchErrorMarker`, which `finalMessage`
(`AppModel.swift:1421-1422`) detects *before* any "No packets"/"Captured N" wording, and
`cageSectionInputs(fromRows: [])` returns `(nil, nil)` so Cage reads UNTESTED. A capture
that never ran does not render as a capture that found nothing.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** depends on **D079** (`DELTAS.md:29269`).
**Fix(es):** Not a one-liner, and deliberately not fixed in the D079 pass — it needs a
security decision, because the target binary lives in a user-writable app bundle and the
helper runs as root. Options, in order of preference:
[Fix 1] Have the helper exec a root-owned copy of the CLI staged next to itself in
`/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/` (not user-writable, so no privilege-escalation window),
staged at bless time.
[Fix 2] Add a `cliPath` parameter to `SyxxPrivilegedHelperProtocol.startCageCapture` and
have the helper require, before `Process.run()`, that the path passes a
`SecStaticCode` requirement equal to the `SMAuthorizedClients` string in
`DeepSyxx-Info.plist:19` — never exec an unvalidated client-supplied path as root.
[Fix 3] Do the capture in the helper itself and drop the re-exec entirely.
Whichever is chosen, add a test that the helper's resolved CLI path is not derived from
`Bundle.main` and a negative test that an unsigned/mismatched binary is refused.
**Code Sample:**
```swift
// DeepSyxx/main.swift:26-34 — Bundle.main is /Library/PrivilegedHelperTools for a daemon
guard let bundlePath = Bundle.main.bundlePath as String? else { ... }
let syxxPath = "\(bundlePath)/Contents/Resources/syxx"   // never exists once installed
guard FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: syxxPath) else {
    withReply("", -1, "syxx binary not found at \(syxxPath)")
    return
}
```
```swift
// AppModel.swift:1132 — the correct path is computed and then thrown away
guard let bin = Self.locateCageBinary() else { ... }   // warning: 'bin' never used
```

**[D117]**
**Criticality:** HIGH — the privileged helper's entire source tree is outside version control
**Location:** `macOS/Sources/DeepSyxx/` (untracked), `macOS/Package.swift:76`
**Issue:** `git ls-files macOS/Sources/DeepSyxx/` returns nothing and
`git status --porcelain --ignored` reports the directory as `?? ` — untracked, not ignored.
`main.swift`, `DeepSyxx-Info.plist`, `com.milkandgin.deepsyxx.plist` and
`DeepSyxx.entitlements` exist only on this machine. `Package.swift:76` declares the
`DeepSyxx` executable target unconditionally, so a fresh clone cannot build the package at
all, and no CI leg can ever have compiled the helper — which is why a packaging defect of
D079's severity survived to a shipped bundle. The security-critical plists
(`SMAuthorizedClients`, `MachServices`) are also unreviewable and unrevertible.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** enables **D079**, **D116**.
**Fix(es):** `git add macOS/Sources/DeepSyxx/` and add a CI leg that builds the `DeepSyxx`
product and asserts `otool -l` reports both `__info_plist` and `__launchd_plist`.

## Cross-platform witness round-trip audit — 2026-07-29 (syxx-engineer)

Both entries below were CONFIRMED by running the real macOS and Windows CLIs against each
other's real output on 2026-07-29, not by reading code. Both are FIXED in this pass; the
acceptance bar recorded with each is a real bidirectional round trip, and both directions
were observed to complete.

---

**[B252]**
**Criticality:** HIGH — a whole block of evidence inside a document Syxx labels "Tamper-evident"
is neither signed nor checked, and can be rewritten to say anything while the tool still prints
VALID.
**Location:** `macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/SignedEvidencePayload.swift:33-36`, `:190` (`dohOutcomes:
nil`); `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/SignedEvidencePayload.cs:38-44`, `:175`;
`macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/WitnessSigner.swift:262-274` and
`Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/WitnessSigner.cs:271-296` (the display-integrity rebuild, which never
looks at `dohOutcomes`).
**Issue:** `ComparisonResult.dohOutcomes` is written into `witness.json` and rendered to the
user, but `SignedEvidencePayload.build`/`Build` always sets the canonical `dohOutcomes` to nil,
so the DoH observations are not covered by the Ed25519 signature; and because the D083/B037
display-integrity guard rebuilds only what the canonical payload contains, they are not covered
by that guard either. Found while running the negative controls for B251. Demonstrated on BOTH
platforms against a real macOS-signed snapshot: rewriting `comparison.dohOutcomes.Cloudflare`
from `{"status":"transportError","records":[]}` to
`{"status":"success","records":[{"rdata":"6.6.6.6",...}]}` still reports:

      Verification: VALID (Tamper-evident — Ed25519 signed)

A related, narrower instance: because the canonical payload records only a per-resolver
`success` boolean (`!didFail`) rather than the typed status, swapping one non-failing status for
another (e.g. `nxDomain` -> `success`) on a resolver with no records is also undetectable, since
neither the boolean nor the answer sets change.
**Honesty note:** the DoH block is presented to the user inside the same document as the signed
evidence with no visual distinction between what the signature covers and what it does not, so a
reader has no way to know which half is attested.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** sits directly on the **E011** canonical-payload contract; found during the
**B251** fix. Related to **B037** (per-resolver outcomes are signed evidence too), which made
the UDP outcomes tamper-evident but left the DoH ones out.
**Fix(es):** NOT fixed in this pass, deliberately. Covering `dohOutcomes` changes the signed
bytes and therefore needs a `formatVersion` bump with verifier-before-signer migration on both
platforms (the pattern `SignedEvidencePayload.cs:96-101` already documents for v3), plus a
user-visible wording decision about how an unattested section is labelled — neither is a minimal
change and the wording needs approval. Options, in order of preference:
[Fix 1] Populate the canonical `dohOutcomes` (the field already exists on both platforms
precisely so byte-parity holds) under a new format version, and extend the display-integrity
rebuild to compare it.
[Fix 2] Carry the typed status in the canonical outcome instead of the bare `success` boolean,
closing the narrower `nxDomain` -> `success` instance at the same time. Note this collides with
the **E020** direction of removing platform-divergent free text from signed bytes; the status is
an enum, not free text, so it is safe to sign where the error string is not.
[Fix 3] Until either lands, render the unsigned sections distinctly so the user is not invited
to trust them as attested.
**Characterisation tests (deliberately assert the CURRENT, DEFECTIVE behaviour so that fixing
B252 turns them red and forces a deliberate update):**
`macOS/Tests/SyxxKitTests/WitnessCrossPlatformGoldenVectorTests.testKnownGap_B252_rewritingDoHEvidenceStillReadsValid`
and `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Tests/WitnessCrossPlatformGoldenVectorTests.KnownGap_B252_RewritingDoHEvidenceStillReadsValid`.

**[B253]**
**Criticality:** HIGH — `syxx witness verify` exits 0 on Windows for a TAMPERED snapshot, so any
script, CI gate, or automation that checks the exit code reads forged evidence as verified.
**Location:** `Windows/dotNET/Syxx/Commands/WitnessCommand.cs:246-300` (the verify branch never
assigns `Environment.ExitCode`); `macOS/Sources/SyxxCLI/SyxxCLI.swift:7-8`, `:15`, `:196-202`.
**Issue:** Found while establishing the B251 round-trip acceptance bar. Measured on this host,
2026-07-29, against real snapshots:

    Windows  witness verify <valid file>                  -> exit 0
    Windows  witness verify <display-mismatch tamper>     -> exit 0     <-- false clean
    Windows  witness verify <unreadable / malformed>      -> exit 0     <-- false clean
    macOS    witness verify <valid file>                  -> exit 2
    macOS    witness verify <display-mismatch tamper>     -> exit 2
    macOS    witness report / nslook / witness --help     -> exit 2

Windows reports success for every outcome including forgery. macOS reports 2 for every outcome
including success, so the code carries no information there either — but at least never claims
clean. The printed text is correct and honest on both platforms (`VALID` vs `DISPLAY MISMATCH`);
it is only the machine-readable channel that lies, which is the channel automation reads.
macOS additionally does not honour its OWN documented contract: `SyxxCLI.swift:7` states
"0=clean, 1=threat, 2=inconclusive, 3=error" and `:8` initialises `exitCode = 2`, but only the
guided-mode synthesis path (`:196-202`) ever assigns it, so every subcommand — including
`--help` — exits 2.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** found during the **B251** cross-platform round-trip verification. Same
honesty spine as **D099** (which fixed the printed text for exactly this command, but not the
exit status).
**Fix(es):** NOT fixed here — it is CLI-surface work outside this pass's scope and the mapping is
a user-visible contract that needs approval.
[Fix 1] Map `SnapshotVerification` onto the documented codes on BOTH platforms: `Valid` -> 0;
`InvalidSignature` / `DisplayMismatch` -> 1 (tampered); `Expired` / `Stale` / `FutureDated` /
`UntrustedSigner` / `MalformedInput` / `UnsupportedFormat` -> 2 (inconclusive, not clean, not
tampered); an I/O or parse failure -> 3.
[Fix 2] Make macOS's per-subcommand paths set `exitCode` rather than inheriting the default, and
add a test per subcommand that asserts the code, since a default that is never overwritten is
indistinguishable from a deliberate one.
[Fix 3] Document the mapping in the `--help` text so scripting against it is supportable.

**STATUS 2026-07-29 (syxx-engineer): PARTIALLY FIXED — Fix 1 landed on both platforms, Fix 2 landed
for `witness` only, Fix 3 NOT landed (needs owner approval for the `--help` wording).**

Fix 1 implemented at `macOS/Sources/SyxxCLI/Commands/SyxxWitness.swift:16-43`
(`WitnessExitStatus` + `forVerification`) and its C# twin
`Windows/dotNET/Syxx/Commands/WitnessCommand.cs:30-67` (`ExitStatus` + `ForVerification`), wired to
the process at `macOS/Sources/SyxxCLI/SyxxCLI.swift:38` and via `Environment.ExitCode` at every
`return` in `WitnessCommand.RunAsync`. `--help`/`--version` now exit 0
(`SyxxCLI.swift:52-57`). Windows' unknown-subcommand code changed from 1 (which the contract reads
as THREAT DETECTED) to 3, matching macOS `SyxxCLI.swift:58-60` (`Program.cs:81-91`).

Measured AFTER the fix, both CLIs against the SAME files, including a snapshot produced by the
Windows CLI and verified by both:

    file                    outcome              macOS  Windows
    valid.json              VALID                    0        0
    tampered-display.json   DISPLAY MISMATCH         1        1
    tampered-sig.json       INVALID SIGNATURE        1        1
    valid.json --max-age 0  STALE                    2        2
    malformed.json          unreadable               3        3
    nonexistent.json        unreadable               3        3
    win-made.json           VALID                    0        0
    win-made-tampered.json  INVALID SIGNATURE        1        1
    witness --help / syxx --help / --version         0        0
    witness (usage) / witness bogus / bad subcmd     3        3

Printed text unchanged on both platforms. Regression tests spawn the REAL binary and assert on the
PROCESS exit code (a unit test on `ForVerification` would not have caught this defect, because
nothing was ever wired to the process): `macOS/Tests/SyxxKitTests/WitnessVerifyExitCodeTests.swift`
(8 tests) and `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Tests/WitnessVerifyExitCodeTests.cs` (8 tests). Mutation-verified
RED: reverting the wiring fails 8/8 macOS and 5/8 Windows; mapping `DisplayMismatch -> Clean` fails
exactly the tamper test on both.

**REMAINDER OF B253, STILL OPEN.** Fix 2 is done for `witness` only. The other 14 macOS subcommands
(`nslook`, `mirror`, `leak`, `key`, `route`, `lan`, `forensics`, `sentinel`, `cage`, `warp`,
`speed`, `unbound`, `filter`, `firstrun`) still inherit the default `exitCode = 2`
(`SyxxCLI.swift:8`), so their exit status still carries no information — honest ("no
determination") but useless to automation, and `syxx mirror --help` still exits 2. Fix 3 is
unstarted: the mapping is undocumented in `--help`, so scripting against it is unsupportable until
the wording is approved.

**[B255]**
**Criticality:** MEDIUM — a network-dependent test reports a product regression when the host's DNS
is degraded, so the suite's red/green signal is not trustworthy on a degraded network
**Location:** `macOS/Tests/SyxxAppTests/AppModelWitnessTests.swift:288-295`
**Issue:** `testRunWitnessThreadsRealErrorNotGenericString` plants a deliberately malformed signing
key and asserts the failure message starts with `"Could not create witness snapshot"`. It performs
REAL DNS lookups (the file header says so at `:17-24`). The author anticipated the no-network case
and added an `XCTSkip` — but the skip is inside `guard case .failed(let msg) = ...`, so it only
fires when the state is NOT `.failed`. On this host the degraded path DOES reach `.failed`, with
`"Witness failed: vantage comparison did not return a result."`, which slips past the guard and
fails the prefix assertion. The guard's own comment describes exactly this outcome and calls it "a
pass for this delta", so the intent is clear and the implementation does not match it.
Observed 2026-07-29 across six full `swift test` runs on an unchanged binary: FAIL, FAIL, then four
consecutive PASS; the same test run alone six times passed 6/6. It only reddens under full-suite
load, when many tests contend for DNS at once on a host where SyxxKit's UDP resolution times out
while `dig` succeeds.
**NOT SOFTENED, NOT SKIPPED, NOT TOUCHED (AGENTS.md Protocol §4).** Filed rather than "fixed": the
assertion itself is correct and worth keeping, and making a flaky honesty test quiet is the failure
mode this project already suffered from. This is a TEST-DESIGN error, not a code defect — the
product behaved honestly in both outcomes (a real error was surfaced, with a real message, in both
the signing-failure and the vantage-failure case).
**Fix(es):**
[Fix 1] Move the network-degradation branch OUT of the `guard`: after binding `msg`, skip (or assert
the weaker "is non-generic and names a real cause" property) when the message matches the
vantage-failure path, and keep the strict prefix assertion for the signing-failure path.
[Fix 2] Better: give `VantageComparer` an injection seam so this test can drive the signing failure
without any network at all. The file header already records that no seam exists and that adding one
was out of scope for the pass that wrote it.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** found while landing **B253** / **D118**; unrelated to both — neither change
touches `AppModel`'s testify path or `VantageComparer`.

**[B256]**
**Criticality:** LOW-MEDIUM — a wall-clock-timing test flakes under parallel test load, so the
Windows suite's red/green signal is not fully trustworthy
**Location:** `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Tests/MonitorSchedulerTests.cs:80-101`
**Issue:** `StartRunsImmediately_ThenStopHalts` drives `MonitorScheduler` at a 0.02s interval and
asserts, after `await Task.Delay(90)` (~4 intervals), that `TickCount > 1`. Both the interval and
the wait are bare wall-clock delays with no slack, so on a loaded machine the scheduler can be
starved long enough that only the immediate `Start()` tick lands and the final
`Assert.True(afterStop > 1, ...)` fails.
Observed 2026-07-29: 1 failure in 4 consecutive full-suite runs of an UNCHANGED binary
(runs 1, 3, 4 green; run 2 red). Non-deterministic on fixed code means environmental, not a product
defect — the scheduler behaved correctly, it was simply not given CPU.
**NOT SOFTENED, NOT SKIPPED, NOT TOUCHED (AGENTS.md Protocol §4).** The assertion is testing a real
property (`Start()` must tick immediately, `Stop()` must actually halt the loop) and is worth
keeping. Filed rather than quietly widened.
**Fix(es):**
[Fix 1] Replace the wall-clock wait with a signal: have the tick callback release a
`SemaphoreSlim`/`TaskCompletionSource` and `await` the second tick with a generous timeout, so the
test asserts "the loop ticked again" rather than "the loop ticked again within 90ms".
[Fix 2] Failing that, widen the interval-to-wait ratio substantially (e.g. 0.02s interval, 500ms
wait) so scheduler starvation cannot flip it.
[Fix 3] Mark the test's xUnit collection as non-parallel, so it is not competing with the rest of
the suite for threads while it measures time.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** same class as **B255** (macOS side): a test whose red/green depends on the
host rather than on the product. Found while establishing the **B253** acceptance bar; unrelated to
that change, which touches neither the scheduler nor its test.

**[D122]**
**Criticality:** MEDIUM — one platform's secure-resolution path is invisible in telemetry
**Location:** `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Kit/DNSResolver.cs:305-307`
(`ResolveSecurelyAsync`); macOS twin `macOS/Sources/SyxxKit/DNSResolver.swift:300-310`
**Issue:** macOS `DNSResolver.resolveSecurely` wraps its body in
`SyxxTracer.span("dns.resolve.secure", kind: .client, ...)` with a `classify` closure that carries
the response's own tri-state onto the span. The Windows twin `ResolveSecurelyAsync` emits NO span
at all. `dns.resolve.secure` is the only span name that exists on one platform and not the other.
Every dashboard, alert or SLO built on that span covers macOS only, and the DoH secure-resolution
path — including its inconclusive outcomes, which are exactly what this product must surface —
is unobservable on Windows.
**OPEN — NOT FIXED, deliberately.** Found by the new parity gate (**D120**) on its first real run;
the fix is production code in `Syxx.Kit`, outside the QA lane's scope. Confirmed to be the ONLY
outstanding divergence: applying the one-line span in an isolated source copy took the gate from
exit 1 to exit 0 with no other change. Required fix: instrument `ResolveSecurelyAsync` with
`SyxxTracer.StartSpan("dns.resolve.secure", ActivityKind.Client, ...)` and set the outcome from the
returned `SecureResolutionResponse.Result` so an `Inconclusive` response is not reported as a
success span. Until then the `parity` job is RED on this, correctly.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** detected by **D120**.

**[D123]**
**Criticality:** LOW — a timing-dependent test that fails only under full-suite load
**Location:** `Windows/dotNET/Syxx.Tests/MonitorSchedulerTests.cs:100`
(`StartRunsImmediately_ThenStopHalts`)
**Issue:** Failed in the baseline full-suite run ("the loop ran more than the immediate tick",
228 passed / 1 failed / 229 total) but passed 3/3 times when run in isolation, and passed in the
subsequent full-suite run (232/232). The assertion is sensitive to scheduler wall-clock timing
under parallel load, so it reports a scheduling delay as a product defect. A test that fails
intermittently for reasons unrelated to what it claims to check trains the team to ignore red.
**OPEN — NOT FIXED.** Recorded rather than silenced: it was NOT retried-into-green, weakened or
skipped. Note this contradicts the Phase 7.1 brief's premise that the Windows suite was "221
passing" and green; the measured baseline was 229 total with 1 failure.
**Cross-ref. Delta:** none.
